In the early 2000s, non-linear2 dimensionality reduction methods were extremely popular to solve
the manifold learning problem. For instance, Laplacian Eigenmaps (LE) [20] use spectral techniques
to compute embeddings, and IsoMap [169] use a combination of the Floyd–Warshall algorithm and
the classical Multi-dimensional scaling algorithm to preserve global graph geodesics. While these
methods have had critical impact on important applications such as RNA sequencing [126], they do
not scale to large datasets. For instance, IsoMAP requires computing all pairs of shortest paths which
takes quadratic time. A perhaps more important limitation of non-linear dimensionality reduction
methods is their inability to compute embeddings for new datapoints because the mappings from
node to embeddings are non-parametric.
In more recent years, graph embedding grew into its own area of research, due to the widespread
prevalence of graph-structured data in many applications such as computational chemistry [76],
biology [162], recommendation systems [106], semi-supervised learning [75]. Many ML and deep
learning models have been proposed for the problem of graph embedding, and these methods are
often referred to using the umbrella term of Graph Representation Learning (GRL) (see Chapter 2).
In contrast with manifold learning applications where the graph structure is latent and inferred from
high-dimensional data, graphs in GRL applications can be directly read from the data (e.g., user
links in a social network). Because they leverage the expressiveness of deep neural networks, GRL
models often yield more expressive, scalable and generalizable embeddings than classical methods.
In most GRL models [79, 102, 142], embeddings are learned in low-dimensional Euclidean spaces,
in which the vector-space structure makes computations extremely easy (e.g., straight-line geodesics).
