Our goal in this thesis is to develop models for graph-structured data that exhibits non-Euclidean
structures (Fig. 1.3) by using alternative embedding geometries.3 In particular, we focus on graphs
with hierarchical and tree-like structures which arise in many core areas of computer science including
computer vision [188], biology [8, 151], phylogenetics [25], natural language processing [112, 168]
and internet [130, 133]. These hierarchical graphs can be represented with much better fidelity in
hyperbolic spaces [152]. Intuitively, this is because hyperbolic spaces can be thought of as continuous
versions of trees, where geodesics (generalization of shortest paths on manifolds) resemble shortest
paths in discrete trees. Therefore, embedding hierarchical graphs in curved spaces that best match
the geometry of the data yields significantly better-quality embeddings.
Leveraging hyperbolic spaces in ML applications comes with important challenges. First, in the
representation learning step, one needs to define objective functions in terms of geodesics which are
no longer simple straight lines, optimize embeddings with Riemannian optimization, and generalize
