1.2 Euclidean Embeddings and the Curse of Dimensionality
The goal in GRL is to seek mappings from nodes in graphs to points in a continuous space (e.g.
Euclidean) which preserve the graph information. Such mappings are called embeddings, a terminology
that was borrowed from differential geometry and the theory of metric spaces. Formally, an embedding
between metric spaces is defined as follows.
Definition 1.2.1 (Embedding). Let (X, dX ) and (Y, dY ) denote two metric spaces. A mapping
f : X → Y is called an embedding.
Given a complicated metric space (X, dX ) (e.g. metric derived from biological DNA sequences),
the metric embedding problem is to find a simpler metric description (e.g. tree metric or Euclidean
metric) via an embedding of (X, dX ). This embedding should preserve distances in the original
metric space as best as possible. It is not always possible to find an isometric (distance-preserving)
embedding, and the quality of embeddings can be measured via the worst-case distortion:
