The graph embedding problem can then be thought of as a metric embedding problem, where the
input metric space X is simply a graph metric, and the output metric space is a simpler metric space
such as the Euclidean space in small dimensions.
This metric embedding problem has been extensively studied in theoretical computer science, one
motivation being the design of algorithms in simplified metric spaces. For instance, many problems
(e.g. nearest neighbors, sparsest cuts, max cuts) deal with distance measures between items, and
designing efficient algorithms for any arbitrary metric can be challenging.
In contrast, if the metric comes from a simplified geometric space such as the Euclidean space,
efficient algorithms are easier to design. Examples of problems that can be solved efficiently using
low-dimensional Euclidean embeddings include nearest neighbor search. For instance, k-d trees are
space-partitioning data structures based on Euclidean coordinates and that can be leveraged to
efficiently find nearest neighbors in low-dimensions.
Therefore, a natural question is: can we embed any arbitrary metric in the Euclidean space with
low-distortion? Bourgain’s fundamental result says the following.
Theorem 1.2.1 (Bourgain, 1985). Every metric space on n points embeds in (RO(log2 n), || · ||p) with
distortion O(log n).


