The embedding in Bourgain’s theorem is far from being isometric (distance-preserving) for large
inputs. We may therefore ask a follow-up question: can we always decrease the distortion of Euclidean
embeddings by increasing the number of dimensions? The answer is no and we’ll see an example next.
Example 1.2.1. Consider for instance a simple graph metric on four nodes in Fig. 1.4, (G, dG),
and assume each edge has length one. It is not possible to embed this metric isometrically into the
Euclidean space (Rd, || · ||2)) of any dimension. We can prove this claim by contradiction: assume
that there exist an isometric embedding f : G → Rd. We get: ||f (a) − f (d)||2 = ||f (a) − f (b)||2 +
||f (b) − f (d)||2. Therefore, (a, b, d) are colinear in Rd. Similarly, we get that (a, b, c) are colinear
in Rd which implies that ||f (c) − f (d)||2 = 0. This contradicts the fact that dG(c, d) = 2, which
completes the proof.
Remark 1.2.1. This result in fact holds for arbitrary tree metrics, i.e. the Euclidean space of any
dimension cannot embed (nontrivial) trees with low distortion [150, 152].
Remark 1.2.2. The example shown in Fig. 1.4 is a specific case of a tree metric, or more generally
a 0-hyperbolic metric in the sense of Gromov [78]. We will see in Section 1.3 how this property ties
nicely to the geometry of the hyperbolic space, which can be used to represent trees with arbitrarily
low distortion in just two dimensions [152].
Therefore, it is not always possible to find low-distortion embeddings of graphs by increasing
the dimensionality of the embedding space. Furthermore, for situations where this may be possible,
increasing the dimensionality too much may cause issues due to the curse of dimensionality.
For instance, in the specific case of high-dimensional Euclidean spaces, the data becomes very
sparse and distances concentrate to the same value. This causes many issues in downstream
applications such as nearest neighbor search, clustering, or ML prediction tasks. For instance, the
performance of k-d tree-based nearest neighbor search algorithms significantly degrades in high
dimensions due to the curse of dimensionality. In the context of ML, working with high-dimensional
may cause optimization issues and overfitting, thus the need to find low-dimensional representations.
This therefore motivate the need for low-distortion and low-dimensional representations.


