1.3 Hyperbolic Embeddings
We saw that Euclidean embeddings may not always yield low-distortion for certain graph structures
such as trees. In particular, if we think of a graph as the discrete approximation of some smooth
manifold, then this graph will isometrically embed in the Euclidean space only if the underlying
manifold is intrinsically Euclidean. However, graphs are complex relational structure that may not
always have a Euclidean geometry, and it is therefore natural to ask the following: can we identify
topological structures in graphs that correspond to certain underlying geometries, and can we use
these geometries to learn better embeddings of graphs? The answer is yes, specifically for the case of
cycle and tree-like graphs, which have nice correspondences with spherical and hyperbolic geometries.
In this dissertation, our focus will be on the latter, and we will study different representation
learning models and downstream algorithms that operate in hyperbolic spaces. In contrast with
Euclidean spaces which have zero curvature, and spherical spaces which have constant positive
curvature, hyperbolic spaces have constant negative curvature (Fig. 1.5). This induces very tight
connections with tree-like graphs, making them ideal candidates to embed hierarchical data. We
will discuss these connections in Section 1.3.2 but first start by giving a brief review of the Poincaré
model of hyperbolic space in Section 1.3.1.
We assume familiarity with basic concepts of differential and Riemannian geometry, including
notions like metric tensors, tangent spaces and exponential maps. For the reader that is not familiar
with these concepts, we provide a brief review of Riemannian geometry in Appendix A, and we refer
to standard texts [36, 113] for a more in-depth treatment.


