Definition 1.3.1 (Gromov product). In any metric space (X, d), the Gromov product of points
x, y ∈ X with respect to a third point z ∈ X is:
〈x, y〉z = 1
2 (d(x, z) + d(z, y) − d(x, y)) .
When the base point z is taken to be the origin or the root of a tree, we shorten this to 〈x, y〉
unambiguously.
A key characterization of hyperbolic spaces is the notion of δ-hyperbolicity, which essentially
measures how close a metric is to a tree metric.
Definition 1.3.2 (δ-hyperbolicity, four-point condition). A metric space (X, d) is δ-hyperbolic if
there exists δ ≥ 0 such that for all w, x, y, z in X:
〈x, y〉z ≥ min{〈x, w〉z, 〈w, y〉z} − δ.
Up to changing δ by a constant multiple, there are many equivalent variations of the notion of
δ-hyperbolicity. In particular, one intuitive interpretation of δ-hyperbolic spaces is using the notion
of δ-slim triangles, which says that any triangle in a δ-hyperbolic space has distance from any side to
the other two less than δ.
Example 1.3.1. The Euclidean space Rd is not δ-hyperbolic and Euclidean triangles are not δ-slim.
For instance the midpoint of a large isosceles triangle might be far from the other two sides (Fig. 1.7a).
Example 1.3.2. Metric trees are 0-hyperbolic (Fig. 1.7b).
Therefore, metric spaces with smaller δ will be close to a tree metric (Fig. 1.7c). This is the case
for hyperbolic spaces which are δ-hyperbolic in the sense of Gromov.
Example 1.3.3. The hyperbolic space is log 3-hyperbolic.
Connections between trees and hyperbolic spaces Now let’s take another look at Exam
ple 1.2.1. We proved that a small tree on four nodes cannot be embedded into the Euclidean space
of any dimension (in fact this generally holds for any nontrivial tree). Looking at the notion of
delta-hyperbolicity, we see that hyperbolic spaces are much more similar to tree metric spaces, due to
the delta-hyperbolic condition. In particular, the behaviour of geodesics in δ-slim triangles resembles
that of shortest paths in trees: the distance between two points in a slim triangle approaches the
sum of distances to the center of the triangle, similar to trees where the shortest path between two
nodes in a triangle is the sum of distances to the center or root of the tree.


