More generally, there are important properties such as quasi-isometries between δ-hyperbolic
spaces and tree metric spaces. First, any finite set of points in a δ-hyperbolic space can be embedded
in a tree metric with bounded distortion:
Proposition 1.3.1 (Tree-likeness of hyperbolic space). There is a constant Cn = δ · O(n) such that
for any set of n points x1, . . . , xn in a δ-hyperbolic space (M, dM ) can be embedded via f : M → T
into a tree metric (T, dT ) such that:
∀i, j : dM (xi, xj) ≤ dT (f (xi), f (xj)) ≤ dM (xi, xj) + Cn.
Conversely, Sarkar [152] derived a construction to embed trees in hyperbolic spaces with arbitrarily
low-distortion in just two-dimensions.
Proposition 1.3.2 (Sarkar). Any unit-weight tree T can be embedded into (B2, dB) with scale
ζ = O(1/ε) and worst-case distortion at most 1 + ε, i.e.
ζdT (i, j) ≤ dB(zi, zj) ≤ ζ(1 + ε)dT (i, j).
These nice quasi-isometries between hyperbolic spaces and tree metrics spaces motivate the use
of hyperbolic geometry to embed tree-like graphs with low-distortion. Further, because we have
mapping from and to trees, these hyperbolic spaces can be thought as some continuous version of
trees. We will leverage these properties in Chapter 5 to continuously search the discrete space of
discrete binary trees with gradient-based optimization.
Exponential volume growth Finally, another intuitive property of hyperbolic spaces which
illustrates their connections to trees is the exponential volume growth. Thinking again of trees as
the discrete approximation of some underlying hyperbolic manifold, we can define a notion of volume
in trees which as the number of nodes contained within some bounded distance (the radius) to the
root of the tree. This volume grows exponentially as we increase the radius (Fig. 1.6b).
In Euclidean space, the volume of balls grows polynomially as a function of the radius, making it
less suitable to embed exponentially growing graphs like trees. More concretely, because the growth
is not as fast as that of the embedded graph, the embedding space quickly becomes too crowded (e.g.
geodesics intersect each other and cause high distortion.
In contrast, δ-hyperbolic spaces, and in particular hyperbolic spaces have exponential growth,
which yields more room to fit complex hierarchies and makes these spaces ideal candidates to embed
trees while accommodating for their exponential volume growth (Fig. 1.6c).


