1.4 Contributions and Outline
We now move provide an overview of our key contributions. As mentioned in Section 1.1, ML generally
follow a two step paradigm: representation learning and downstream tasks such as classification,
clustering or data visualization.
Our contribution in this dissertation follows this paradigm. First, we propose GRL models that
learn representations for hierarchical graphs in hyperbolic spaces (Section 1.4.1). Second, we design
algorithms that work with hyperbolic inputs for important downstream applications: hierarchical
clustering and dimensionality reduction (Section 1.4.2).
1.4.1 Part I: Embeddings
A core aspect of GRL is the underlying embedding space geometry. Graphs are discrete, high
dimensional, non-Euclidean structures, and there is no straightforward way to encode this information
into low-dimensional Euclidean spaces while preserving the graph topology. We discussed the
limitations of Euclidean embeddings in Section 1.2 and motivated the use of hyperbolic geometry to
embed tree-like graphs in Section 1.3. Many real-world graphs such as knowledge graphs, biological
protein-protein interactions networks have hierarchies and it is therefore important to design GRL
models with the right inductive biases for these hierarchical graphs.
Our first contribution in this dissertation is the development of hyperbolic graph embedding models.
In particular, we propose two embedding models for applications that deal with multi-relational
graphs and graphs that come with additional node feature information.
• Chapter 2 is based on the 2019 review paper “Machine Learning on Graphs: A Model and
Comprehensive Taxonomy” [39]. In this chapter, we describe the Graph Representation Learning
(GRL) problem in more details, and provide a technical review of different GRL algorithms that
will be used throughout this dissertation. We provide a comprehensive framework, GraphEDM,
for GRL and discuss key notions such as inductive and transductive learning, supervised and
unsupervised GRL, deep and shallow encoders, and we discuss the role of node features in GRL.
The GraphEDM framework connects many areas of GRL, including traditional manifold learning
methods and more recent deep neural networks for graph-structured data. To illustrate its gener
ality, we fit over thirty existing methods into this framework, including Poincaré embeddings [136]
and Graph Convolutional Networks (GCNs) [102].
• Chapter 3 is based on the NeurIPS 2019 paper “Hyperbolic Graph Convolutional Neural Net
works” [38]. In this chapter, we generalize neural network operations such as neighborhood
aggregation to hyperbolic spaces and propose Hyperbolic Graph Convolutional Neural Network
(HGCN), the first hyperbolic GCN that leverages both the expressiveness of GCNs and hyperbolic
geometry to learn node representations for hierarchical graphs. In contrast with existing shallow


