1.4.2 Part II: Algorithms
GRL methods learn graph representations in a continuous embedding space. These representations
can then be used as features for downstream prediction tasks, or for data visualization and analysis.
When representations come from non-Euclidean spaces such as the hyperbolic space, crucial ML
algorithms such as clustering, nearest neighbor search and dimensionality reduction do not easily
generalize to the hyperbolic case since they rely on fundamental axioms of Euclidean geometry (e.g.
shortest paths between points are straight lines). This motivates the need for algorithms that operate
in hyperbolic spaces, which is our second main contribution.
• Chapter 5 is based on the NeurIPS 2020 paper “From Trees to Continuous Embeddings and Back:
Hyperbolic Hierarchical Clustering” [40]. In this work, we propose an algorithm to hierarchically
cluster data in hyperbolic spaces with provable guarantees. These clusters can be visualized
to understand properties of hyperbolic embeddings, or to detect communities in networks. In
particular, our decoding algorithm can be used in conjunction with an encoding algorithm
to continuously search the discrete space of binary trees with gradient-based optimization for
similarity-based Hierarchical Clustering (HC). This work is the first continuous relaxation of
Dasgupta’s discrete optimization for HC [62] with provable quality guarantees. Experimentally,
we compare our approach to agglomerative heuristics on a variety of HC benchmarks and find
that even approximate solutions found with gradient descent have superior clustering quality.
