2.4.1.2 Distance-based: non-Euclidean methods
The distance-based methods described so far assumed embeddings are learned in a Euclidean space.
Graphs are non-Euclidean discrete data structures, and as discussed in Section 1.3, Euclidean
geometry may not always be the best choice to match the structure of input graphs.
In particular, many network studies showed that real-world networks have a underlying hyperbolic
geometry. Kleinberg [103] proposed a greedy algorithm for geometric rooting, which maps nodes in
sensor networks to coordinates on a hyperbolic plane via spanning trees, and then performs greedy
geographic routing. Hyperbolic geometry has also been used to study the structural properties of
complex networks (networks with non-trivial topological features used to model real-world systems).
Krioukov et al. [108] developed a geometric framework to construct scale-free networks (a family of
complex networks with power-law degree distributions), and conversely showed that any scale-free
graph with some metric structure has an underlying hyperbolic geometry. Papadopoulos et al.
[139] introduced the Popularity-Similarity (PS) framework to model the evolution and growth of
complex networks. In this model, new nodes are likely to be connected to popular nodes (modelled
by their radial coordinates in hyperbolic space) as well as similar nodes (modelled by the angular
coordinates). This framework has further been used to map nodes in graphs to hyperbolic coordinates,
by maximising the likelihood that the network is produced by the PS model [140]. Further works
extend non-linear dimensionality reduction techniques such as LLE [20] to efficiently map graphs to
hyperbolic coordinates [7, 128].
In more recent years, Nickel and Kiela [136] proposed a shallow graph embedding model that
learned representations of hierarchical graphs in hyperbolic spaces instead of conventional Euclidean
space using gradient descent and Riemannian optimization [26]. We describe this model using
GraphEDM next.
