2.4.2 Graph Neural Networks
The methods we described so far a shallow in the sense that the mapping from nodes to embeddings
is simply a one-hot encoding and therefore non-parametric. Consequently, these methods can only
learn nodes for embeddings that are seen during training and fail to generalize to new nodes. This
may be a significant downside, especially when learning with dynamic graphs that change over time.
In some scenarios, we may have additional node features as input as discussed in Section 2.2.2.1.
The presence of node features suggests the possibility to learn smooth parametric mappings from
this feature space to embeddings, for instance using feed-froward neural networks, whose weights
can then be learned using gradient descent. However, we also want models that preserve the graph
structure in the embedding space. A possibility is to use a graph regularization loss term to encourage
neighboring nodes in the graph to mapped to points that are close in the embedding space. We call
these methods graph regularization methods and examples include Manifold Regularization [22] and


CHAPTER 2. GRAPH REPRESENTATION LEARNING 35
Planetoid [192].
Another option is to inject relational inductive biases (constraints such as weight sharing) within
neural network architectures to facilitate learning. However, most neural network architectures
have been restricted to simple relational structures such as data that is discretized on Euclidean
domains (e.g. sequential data, or grid data) (Fig. 1.3). For instance, sequential data, such as text or
videos, can be modelled via recurrent neural networks, which can capture sequential information,
yielding efficient representations as measured on machine translation and speech recognition tasks.
Convolutional neural networks (CNNs), which parameterize neural networks according to structural
priors such as shift-invariance, and have achieved unprecedented performance in pattern recognition
tasks such as image classification or speech recognition.
For graph-structured data, it is challenging to define networks with strong structural priors, as
structures can be arbitrary, and can vary significantly across different graphs and even across different
nodes within the same graph. In particular, operations like Euclidean convolutions heavily rely on
geometric priors (such as translation invariance) and cannot be directly applied on irregular graph
domains (e.g. translations may not even be defined on arbitrary graphs). For instance in images,
each pixel has the same neighborhood structure, allowing to apply the same filter weights at multiple
locations in the image. However in graphs, one can’t define an ordering of node since each node
might have a different neighborhood structure (Fig. 1.3).
Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) tackle these challenges and form a family of GRL methods
that inject relational inductive biases in the design of neural networks. GNNs learn differentiable
mappings over discrete graph topological structures and the encoder function can be expressed using
GraphEDM as (Fig. 2.4):
Z = ENC(X, W; ΘE).
