relationship, avoiding precision errors that might arise when using a fixed curvature, and providing
flexibility to encode multiple hierarchies.
4.3 Methodology
The goal of this work is to learn parsimonious hyperbolic embeddings that can encode complex
logical patterns such as symmetry, anti-symmetry, or inversion while preserving latent hierarchies.
Our model, AttH, (1) learns KG embeddings in hyperbolic space in order to preserve hierarchies
(Section 4.3.1), (2) uses a class of hyperbolic isometries parameterized by compositions of Givens
transformations to encode logical patterns (Section 4.3.2), (3) combines these isometries with
hyperbolic attention (Section 4.3.3). We describe the full model in Section 4.3.4 and our optimization
strategy in Section 4.3.5. Before we start, we introduce the problem setup and some notation.
Problem formulation In the KG embedding problem, we are given a set of triples (h, r, t) ∈ E ⊆
V × R × V, where V and R are entity and relationship sets, respectively. The goal is to map entities
v ∈ V to embeddings ev ∈ U dV and relationships r ∈ R to embeddings rr ∈ U dR , for some choice of
space U (traditionally R), such that the KG structure is preserved.
Concretely, the data is split into ET rain and ET est triples. Embeddings are learned by optimizing
a scoring function s : V × R × V → R, which measures triples’ likelihoods. s(·, ·, ·) is trained using
triples in ET rain and the learned embeddings are then used to predict scores for triples in ET est. The
goal is to learn embeddings such that the scores of triples in ET est are high compared to triples that
are not present in E.
Hyperbolic geometry In this chapter, we work with the Poincaré model of hyperbolic space with
negative curvature −c (c > 0): Bd,c = {x ∈ Rd : ||x||2 < 1
