5.4 Hyperbolic Hierarchical Clustering
Our goal is to relax the discrete optimization problem in Eq. (5.2). To do so, we represent trees
using the hyperbolic embeddings of their leaves in the Poincaré disk (Section 5.4.1).2 We introduce a
differentiable HC cost, using a continuous LCA analogue (Section 5.4.2), and then propose a decoding
algorithm, dec(·), which maps embeddings to discrete binary trees (Section 5.4.3). Our main result
is that, when embeddings are optimized over a special set of spread embeddings (Definition 5.4.1),
solving our continuous relaxation yields a (1 + ε)-approximation to the minimizer of Dasgupta’s cost
(Section 5.4.4), where ε can be made arbitrarily small. Next, we present our continuous optimization
framework and then detail the different components of HypHC.
