5.4.1 Continuous Tree Representation via Hyperbolic Embeddings
To perform gradient-based HC, one needs a continuous parameterization of possible binary trees on
a fixed number of leaves. We parameterize binary trees using the (learned) hyperbolic embedding of
their leaves. Our insight is that because hyperbolic space induces a close correspondence between
embeddings and tree metrics [152], the leaf embeddings alone provide enough information to recover
the full tree. In fact, as hyperbolic points are pushed towards the boundary, the embedding becomes
more tree-like (Fig. 5.2) [150]. Thus, rather than optimize over all possible tree structures, which
could lead to combinatorial explosion, we embed only the leaf nodes in the Poincaré disk using an
embedding look-up:
